  "userdoc": {
    "kind": "user",
    "methods": {
      "safe()": {
        "notice": "The Safe whose ownership this module mirrors. The only address         allowed to upgrade the module; everything else is permissionless."
      }
    },
    "notice": "The author Safe's ownership history: who was an owner, and from which         block. Installed as a UUPS proxy per author Safe, one proxy per Safe,         exactly like DraftsModuleV1. Why this exists (the short version):   The gate on the publication module asks `Safe.isOwner(signer)` — present   tense. Rotate one owner and every earlier signature over the author's work   stops verifying, even though the work did not change. This module keeps the   missing tense: `isOwnerAt` and its timestamp twin `wasOwnerAt`. How it stays current:   Safe 1.4.1 has no owner-rotation callback. The only on-chain tap is the   transaction guard (`setGuard` is `onlySelf`, so a Safe tx installs it, and   `execTransaction` calls `checkAfterExecution` every time). Every owner   mutation is `onlySelf`, so every rotation passes through the guard, and the   guard — `OwnerHistoryGuard`, a separate contract because a revert there   would brick the Safe — calls `sync()` here.   `sync()` reads the Safe's live owner set and diffs it against the current   era. It is permissionless and a pure function of chain state: the only   thing it can write is what the Safe already says. That is deliberate — it   is also the repair path when the hook is missed (a module enable/disable   window, a guard removed for a Safe upgrade, an out-of-gas hook). The half-open era model:   Era k covers blocks `[startBlock, endBlock)`; `endBlock == 0` means \"still   current\". At the boundary block — the block the Safe rotated in — the new   set is already authoritative and the previous era is already closed. That   matches the chain: the `AddedOwner`/`RemovedOwner` event and the guard's   `checkAfterExecution` are in that block, so a signature verified *in* that   block sees the new set. Half-open makes `isOwnerAt(x, boundary)` and   `isOwnerAt(y, boundary)` give exactly one true answer for the pair that   swapped, and no block belongs to two eras. Storage declaration order is the upgrade contract, same rule as the drafts module (INV-3 in the publication spec): `safe`, `eras`, `tenureBlocks`, `tenureTimes`, `ownersEverSet`, `seen`.",
    "version": 1
  }
